*/J.D. Vance, Catholicism, and the Postliberal Turn/* is an [[id:495f274d-80ec-47a2-93dc-733355ef9b9c][essay]] [[id:f7cc4bac-8a9d-43a2-a76f-4e014ae70405][authored]] by [[id:b6a7dc39-96af-452f-9e30-97f4ff90a7fa][Dermot Roantree]], published in [[id:70662b3e-a2ff-46fa-9f7e-58bfbb1bedb4][Studies: An Irish Quarterly Review]] volume 114, in [[id:209258fd-3cf3-4a9f-b27e-917a56a9f328][August, 2025]].

It seems to be a call-in from a [[id:ef850d98-48cd-4466-9d3c-39b9c9ca3c2d][Catholic perspective]] of [[id:53093363-4792-4e93-bee4-f33c2a832486][Vance]]'s misapplication of [[id:00393586-bac6-4419-9611-b78dc39e66d3][René Girard]]'s theory of the scapegoat to enact [[id:2f32f23e-bff5-4a84-86c7-7c6961b82b69][Carl Schmitt]]'s Nazism, notably a suspension of elections.

* Notes
1. Opening
   1. Opens talking about [[id:a433cac8-930a-4897-bdab-7e02e0920ca2][John F. Kennedy]] winning the [[id:207a6282-5047-45aa-976f-c213cec208e2][U.S. Presidency]]
      1. Marks an end to an [[id:feccd37a-7d3c-4afa-b761-3ca4318e8548][anti-Catholic]] [[id:84c4f7d0-88c2-418a-9c6c-0db1c4f79cde][Kulturkampf]]: a "belief that [[id:ecfbd1e8-340c-4046-9162-51eabc98cfc0][Catholics]] should be excluded from high office as their [[id:56f33b5d-ca5d-4ce6-8cd0-fb182b1dbb7c][allegiance]] to the [[id:2dcb1503-a9f5-429b-a352-77bfa2765b47][State]] will always be [[id:f9fe41e6-f522-4a00-b98f-7af0f30cdd76][subordinate]] to [[id:a8f97087-972a-4483-af5e-72f396304ec3][Rome]]."
   2. From [[id:8e5998b3-5961-4394-af6c-e4b2cf3b2900][1776]] to the [[id:3b07aa07-2ad8-4943-8bc0-937644201ca0][Kennedy Administration]], there were six Catholics on the [[id:a6abe253-9ae0-4214-9506-4dccfaf8a95c][Supreme Court of the United States]]
      1. As the paper's publishing, there are six Catholics on the court, /right now/.
   3. One-third of [[id:ff45a8a3-9c7e-4248-8666-92966f8142a3][Cabinet of the Second Presidency of Donald J. Trump]] is Catholic
      1. [[id:ecad3cb0-5891-48ac-8c83-935bfa8c66ad][Secretary of State of the United States]] [[id:559a6664-7b3a-44ab-a4cd-8e4ace8fbcb7][Marco Rubio]]
      2. [[id:9e16fdb8-35c9-4b8e-91b5-796b8ed897fc][Attorney General of the United States]] [[id:13547657-0a7b-4b8a-b8d3-95a5a262173d][Pam Bondi]]
      3. [[id:aa3af97c-2416-4a2d-ad05-0742025452cb][Director of the Central Intelligence Agency]] [[id:0f6c0359-11b3-4f24-b0a1-a20555614286][John Ratcliffe]]
   4. Leaders in [[id:e1125994-235a-4124-b0e7-7a0c3e72e27a][political foundations]] and [[id:ac5c5da1-ec14-4b5d-aa8f-88471ffdcc5f][political think-tanks]] that support Trump are Catholic:
      1. [[id:4b1e5a6c-7cac-4b06-98a3-4afe7e6998ec][Project 2025]]
      2. [[id:8c38da40-e486-4d42-9aa1-93c0bbfb0270][Leonardo Leo]] at the [[id:da7b4f58-9d66-4558-a698-17f51dd2838b][Federalist Society]]
      3. [[id:e2c86bed-bad5-41f3-8096-c10617ca8eb4][Tim Busch]] at the [[id:4ac0f296-d47a-48b8-942a-3a1566de76d4][Napa Institute]]
      4. [[id:d9a51bf4-3b2a-44c6-a864-f3a3663201a4][Kevin Roberts]] at the [[id:c8791cf6-b21b-466a-a550-a5740204bf92][Heritage Foundation]]
      5. [[id:c3f05b58-ad73-40de-8ffb-2428e39ab9f0][Linda McMahon]] and [[id:c3dbfaaa-7502-4f0c-becd-f12516135f58][Larry Kudlow]] at the [[id:35a04470-66f9-477d-82d4-47086cd9530a][America First Policy Institute]]
	 1. McMahon is now [[id:aaeee8cc-efea-4b78-b61c-6567a90e3ee7][Secretary of Education of the United States]]
   5. Catholics are less than one-quarter of the [[id:c3ed5de7-05d7-454f-a44f-23ed05ab5e52][United States population]]
2. Postliberal conservatism
   1. All mentioned Catholics (except [[id:f8ce099e-7c39-4ee0-8ec0-2b5b8c4390cb][Sonia Sotomayor]]) identify as [[id:90863f89-e22a-4ea1-9cec-79d5d9ffe092][political conservative]]
      1. Many are identifiable as [[id:e82d3857-fcab-4288-ab92-c499aaa49905][postliberal]], even if they don't self-identify that way
   2. Postliberalism is "neither left nor right"
      1. Postliberalism [[id:4b644355-8760-4f8f-b3ce-1722990c866d][contests]] the [[id:e922bef9-6bf2-4a9a-88bb-e199a087ad77][fundamental principles of liberalism]], especially:
	 1. "[[id:6b82e4b9-0ade-4587-87fa-b992555c902f][protection]] of [[id:878f7492-4238-4fa4-8afa-67335b8674e7][individual rights]] and [[id:6650a5e4-91cd-4374-97e2-b79b4071d874][individual freedoms]] through value-neutral [[id:1f6b29d6-2338-4460-957b-22c9a9a866b1][proceduralism]]"
	 2. "[[id:09c1769c-b347-406f-9a36-f106ed2a7222][rejecting]] any specific [[id:fd6ecbed-9bdf-4570-b4b2-5ff7744c0a96][conception]] of [[id:4a2382fb-f933-4018-be6a-98cbaa01a961][good]] or of a [[id:63c163ea-740a-4f77-a2f5-8d7472d7ffe5][just social order]]"
      2. Postliberalism sees these principles as a paradox: maintaining neutrality requires its own set of values, "and these invariably are [[id:265375f8-cc56-40b0-aa94-26669a8ac304][individualist]], [[id:9d8e3c72-5876-4e2b-b220-a0d312500f28][consumerist]], and [[id:008f1d90-49ce-47f4-8bce-19f06b843f37][relativist]]."
      3. [[id:a0fcac57-9a82-4fa5-b41a-48fbfc27a80e][American leftist postliberalism]] rejects the [[id:db3de213-9491-45b9-9d37-8809d5895665][liberal concept]] of the [[id:b767fc38-64cb-4a4d-a778-ed5754fcec82][self]] as an [[id:3b11031a-7c79-4c45-83f8-9ea4e7024318][autonomous individual]].
	 1. Leftist postliberals include [[id:6c32e079-4448-45d0-ab6c-b38d44b861a4][Stanley Hauerwas]], [[id:7de5ad61-6504-49c7-8b50-3226de74feec][Eugene McCarraher]], [[id:becc1beb-8cad-4722-b32b-aac6989fdd33][William Cavanaugh]]
	 2. Leftist postliberals advocate [[id:85a3c8e6-49e0-4c78-b49e-e74c0667caa7][human socialability]] and [[id:7e1f4b25-cac3-48d3-8c3a-de82dd839394][interdependence]], [[id:7d19891e-4967-4aad-805c-94515e17e10e][solidarity]], [[id:3be05569-6ed3-44d2-9c6a-03e0401f5c23][justice]], [[id:4a8cfa16-de35-4e86-813a-8b2fbb8bc2ec][peace]], and [[id:b286e17d-4dce-4877-9139-afea6f56eaf1][care]].
	 3. "bottom-up":
	    1. [[id:26ceb0e8-2746-4adc-858a-6fda54730710][community-level solidarity]]
	    2. [[id:7b413d52-2983-455b-80a4-848e715ddf33][participatory discourse]]
	    3. [[id:edf99d69-953a-4249-afa2-625872d78dd6][criticism of tradition]]
      4. [[id:decbd8ac-dd39-4a1b-8a1e-be3d8bdf6f1d][American right-wing postliberalism]] approach the same paradox differently
	 1. [[id:171c38fc-8e40-40ea-a2b1-b0ad866385a6][restoring]] [[id:f21e5f46-8c95-4654-ab78-3ad1dc6b42a5][religious]] and [[id:538836d9-8c5b-4fa0-a5de-36d3ac477ce2][cultural traditionalism]]
	 2. "The [[id:4f7e1084-59a6-4a2a-8d0a-3409de3cb4b4][parameters]] of a [[id:63c163ea-740a-4f77-a2f5-8d7472d7ffe5][just society]] are clear."
	    1. not [[id:eead71f0-0228-4da1-8c82-5591dfbeed1f][testing]] through discourse
	    2. known through [[id:ba68ba02-4e36-4f08-864c-7a9eaba2d188][tradition]], [[id:f4b4a0e2-cbbb-4ea3-bef3-eef73dcdcec5][natural law]], [[id:e3e67235-b882-44d6-adfb-3b460b3a24c8][right reasoning]]
	    3. imposed through [[id:0bda12be-531c-476e-aad2-7951496bb988][State power]], [[id:a2b75fd7-2913-4a6f-ae01-8ce646a902a8][government institutions]], and the [[id:5deb67ee-b177-4081-8b17-194402e79092][law]]
	    4. [[id:29158b18-c272-4682-846c-61976cd44b94][Patrick Deneen]] may be seen as chief [[id:c266ada0-8c3f-4041-a0c5-33c79dbd4e83][American right-wing postliberal theorist]].
	       1. Calls for "[[id:526c4023-ad7f-4d21-82ed-a5702c99e254][regime change]]"
		  1. "the peaceful but vigorous overthrow of a corrupt and corrupting liberal ruling class and the creation of a postliberal order in which existing political forms can remain in place, as long as a fundamental different ethos informs those institutions and the personnel who populate key offices and positions."
		     1. emsenn: This really speaks to the difficulty [[id:33b630c1-697a-47f9-a7d5-482689c8b078][American leftists]] seem to be having in understanding the threat of American right-wing postliberalism: they are waiting for a violent overthrow that is not planned.
		  2. [[id:ea492e00-5f5b-4913-aac4-3962f28d1b38][Damon Linker]] explains Deneen's regime change further: "replacing the people and institutions that dominate America's cultural, economic, and political life with a new elite willing to eschew liberal norms in service of supposedly higher ideals."
		     1. emsenn: I can't help but hear how this definition is inclusive of the work of most [[id:e56c0a8f-e48c-4e19-923d-812ddbc3cb52][American Marxists]] and [[id:ca6e8f16-e8ea-485f-9c6d-750dcd819bd4][American anarchists]], in a way that it feels like the postliberal theorists recognize more than the leftist theorists, who seem to do worse and worse understanding their [[id:32d03ee7-91de-4dd9-877a-19662158fbd5][liberal bias]] as time goes on.
	    5. Most of "these postliberals" (seems to mean American right-wing) admire [[id:3a298f0f-f17a-4413-bfba-9caf0d632440][Viktor Orbán]]'s [[id:d636e6bd-05fc-4200-9235-665cf436bced][illiberal democracy]] in [[id:437831c3-f4ee-49e5-822e-8171d3ccbb96][Hungary]]:
	       1. installed [[id:0d742432-8630-4ac5-8c2e-f601717c14fc][strongmen]]
	       2. [[id:93cd672e-4426-4aef-afe3-34061a030190][transformed]] [[id:82125876-1a70-43fd-ade1-d5b82bc2930a][democratic culture]] into a means of [[id:e8e33436-20ae-4db2-a0ad-cb66e1e5096e][expanding executive power]]
	    6. American right-wing postliberalism is not exclusively Catholic, but many are, either from family or [[id:2d9709d8-ee7e-483e-b5b7-7d38213aa495][adult conversion to Catholicism]]: Deneen references the role of Catholicism in shaping his [[id:eca3f105-8731-4eb8-b3ec-ee263a2ff6bb][political philosophy]]
	       1. [[id:6b45b3f5-db86-4976-a058-442e750b6ca7][Gladden Pippen]], one of Orbán's advisors
	       2. [[id:dea23969-4f93-4ab6-9097-b71d33ec615c][Sohrab Ahmari]], [[id:a8bc7e3a-cdf7-4cfa-8e4d-a1a5f7eb2931][author]] and [[id:84f91c46-b0df-4b82-9102-8074dce00c2c][columnist]]
	       3. [[id:b561596f-5d2b-4cd8-b9d5-9383eedc189f][Rusty Reno]], [[id:52cc81a3-7ffe-493d-83a7-2e8fd05bdf6c][editor]] of [[id:d89eff05-198a-48f7-9ffc-1bf242b1852e][First Things]]
	       4. [[id:99403753-804f-4e1b-8904-762fe3e11866][Chad Pecknold]], [[id:09a98279-5840-4a10-87f2-45333e64da77][theologian]]
	       5. [[id:d54879d4-4f64-4690-a491-c5189e25a410][Adrian Vermeule]]
		  1. [[id:20297a29-8672-4b2e-8fbd-197ee94ad98b][common-good constitutionalism]]
		     1. [[id:e158e05b-0325-4ced-a401-903c7e5aa3d0][legalistic support]] for [[id:dd5dbd7d-8c20-40f9-900e-20079fe79735][originalism]] and [[id:ad50ff9d-5e08-4dee-9e78-1eb5e35af0ec][procedural liberalism]]
		     2. [[id:5238f373-145c-46ab-a35f-d64c80a127a5][interpreting]] the [[id:42501877-e021-46e8-bc52-67f89d59017b][Constitution]] through [[id:f4b4a0e2-cbbb-4ea3-bef3-eef73dcdcec5][natural law]], [[id:13bcf2c0-eb24-4546-8fd5-4bdc24f449c0][oriented]] toward [[id:3236626e-21aa-46de-b1a2-5bc5d88960fd][Catholic Social Teaching]]
		     3. common-good constitutionalism "does not suffer from a horror of political domination and hierarchy, because it sees that law is parental, a wise teacher and inculcator of good habits. Just authority in rulers can be exercised fro the good of subjects, if necessary even against the subjects' own perceptions of what is best for them - perceptions that may change over time anyway, as the law teaches, habituates, and re-forms them. Subjects will come to thank the rulers whose legal strictures, possibly experienced at first as coercive, encourage subjects to form more authentic desires for the individual and common goods, better habits, and beliefs that better track and promote communal well-being."
			1. emsenn: [[id:6695c423-4b45-4c26-b8d8-393031974571][Aristotelian teleology]] is back on the menu!
			2. emsenn: Here's a quote from Gramsci's Prison Notebooks: "The supremacy of a social group manifests itself in two ways, as 'domination' and as 'intellectual and moral leadership'... The State is the entire complex of practical and theoretical activities with which the ruling class not only justifies and maintains its dominance, but manages to win the active consent of those over whom it rules."
	       6. For American right-wing postliberalism:
		  1. [[id:100708cd-ea4b-4fe4-999e-a8bbb96545df][Catholic philosophy]], [[id:9ac189fa-9acd-4507-bebe-b1d86f26efc7][Catholic political theology]], and [[id:ec8cf094-145d-41b9-a3b4-d103ba5decad][historical forms]] of [[id:92df29c3-0d81-4790-8622-61786d3d1a8a][Catholic leadership]] are [[id:24a3542e-e93e-443b-9bb7-6c2e366bf47a][resources]] for [[id:290fb2b6-4f18-4eba-bf0c-9f107e395005][expressing]] the [[id:e0c340a0-a59e-4c89-8cfc-8f65333f33a7][postliberal turn]] from [[id:5a898ac0-0046-4074-b0d5-ed6b60aac677][liberalism]], [[id:0b34f2e2-a34a-46bd-a850-151e6d78f0ce][gradualist conservatism]], [[id:f20c2b20-a78b-43c3-a993-3d5c0d2f4ac8][neoliberalism]], and [[id:033cf961-01bb-422b-9aeb-c82853a56dfd][libertarianism]].
		  2. Common [[id:100c0c28-4104-4259-8d3d-0d5120e7aade][American right-wing postliberal glossary]]:
		     1. [[id:cad9789c-7261-423c-8d99-48ea105f20fc][Aristotle]]
