		  3. "the rich and varied apprehension of higher things, the glorious pagenant of Catholicism... broaden[s] the political and active imagination" (Vermeule)
3. The conversion of J.D. Vance
   1. converted in [[id:fdfa87ce-6b1c-4109-8065-63bed6a1cbc2][2019ce]]
      1. during the [[id:85b58a31-a9bc-4a56-a651-d9f904f609a6][First Presidency of Donald J. Trump]]
   2. formed [[id:39ba483d-dbf0-4843-9c3e-dea5ab37e50e][friendship]] with [[id:4817b042-d443-4c7a-9934-beb037bee021][Rod Dreher]], an "Orbán [[id:3778e6ad-bff9-41c4-ba80-bd5a3dc96414][apologist]]"
   3. formed [[id:40a4244e-a940-40f0-a4bb-0e6e265e96f3][relationship]] with Deneen and Ahmari, spoken together, along with others
   4. "[Vance's] choice to join the Catholic Church did not interfere [with] but rather accompanied his conversion to [[id:8436944b-2b2e-4ad0-b760-94e341ddc650][Trumpism]]; while he had been very critical of Trump just a few years earlier, he then embraced and became Trump's more presentable face." - [[id:9c716410-6960-45c6-aaaf-c8b71733940e][Massimo Faggioli]]
   5. Vance has [[id:216fe389-d6e7-4f3f-8869-9db1fd24a54e][self-identified]] as on "the postliberal right"
      1. claimed that his "views on [[id:a90e754b-6057-4e41-befb-a062bff1a900][public policy]] and what the optimal [[id:2dcb1503-a9f5-429b-a352-77bfa2765b47][state]] should look like are pretty aligned with Catholic Social Teaching"
4. Influence of [[id:00393586-bac6-4419-9611-b78dc39e66d3][René Girard]]
   1. Vance says [[id:a638636a-fe00-4553-ab87-19b783e21b9a][Peter Thiel]] introduced him to [[id:015206a1-1a30-4e75-906c-d2af810b4a4c][memetic rivalry]], a concept from REne Girard:
      1. "It was [Girard's] related theory of the [[id:2838cb6d-ce06-48a5-ac37-9f76457b1c0c][scapegoat]] - and what it revealed about [[id:74258b61-3f95-4716-b503-9ebebe02dc08][Christianity]] - that made me reconsider my faith."
      2. "[[id:a493f7bd-f53c-47a5-a820-1f232edfee02][human civilisations]]... are often, perhaps even always, founded on a 'scapegoat myth' - an [[id:6a3e79f3-43fc-4f06-954e-e3266d78eeb4][act of violence]] committed against someone who has wronged the broader community, retold as a sort of origin story for the community."
	 1. emsenn: [[id:4d053d51-5f77-4992-90e2-786cb02ac826][Lakota people]] came out of a [[id:ab3f8ba2-bf1f-4316-9d14-c02a8fc71e7b][cave]] because we got tired of eating [[id:9626f276-e5cc-4f4b-86a4-84f536295c7c][Pleurotus pulmonarius]]
      3. Vance notes Girard's differentiating of the Christian story from other traditions
	 1. Christian reveleation reveals the divine to humanity, and also humanity to humanity
	 2. Christ as [[id:d682d1ba-c3ff-42ba-8219-6c46f71f517a][Lamb of God]] who submits to scapegoat shows the [[id:6b3d7eed-eb8d-4d8b-8e90-28478e16a91e][heart of darkness]]
	 3. "We progress by way of violence." - Roantree
	    1. "Disordered, mimetic desire leads to violent conflict,
	       1. "which is eventually contained,
		  1. "though only temporarily,
	       2. "by the warring sides redirecting their violence toward a scapegoat."
	 4. This requires a "truly guilty" scapegoat
	 5. Christ interrupts this process:
