*/Procedural liberalism/* is [[id:5a898ac0-0046-4074-b0d5-ed6b60aac677][liberalism]] that [[id:790ed6cd-96f2-45e1-858d-09cc9ce6c0fa][prioritizes]] the liberalism of its [[id:e09775e2-ed27-45eb-9e3b-1e12a2150bfc][decision-making procedures]] over the liberalism of its [[id:26073108-7de4-4986-b091-94860c5e1d18][decisions]] or [[id:6141ebe4-2d06-405b-b32d-f4aa87c44c5c][outcomes]].


* History
- [[id:d5689ba1-698a-4ce3-a3ac-f3c44a57ae7b][1971ce]] :: [[id:6221f732-0c58-4509-a9ee-1738d7df3b98][John Rawls]], [[id:450e2402-90b1-412c-9604-ad5967de6568][A Theory of Justice]], introduces "justice as fairness"
- [[id:b48656ef-a88f-4d89-95c7-5961b73a3d1e][1980ce]] :: [[id:5dbfbb51-8076-492e-9e33-7d7dbbcd8080][Bruce Ackerman]], [[id:2fc4cc63-c565-4b3f-924e-1b614d92d6fa][Social Justice in the Liberal State]], argues for [[id:2eed6878-20f0-4d8b-b4b3-c3a11bdfc16c][procedural neutrality]] in [[id:c87b062a-f43a-4e0f-8cb5-943fe815cf09][liberal institutions]]
- [[id:a6d4cc29-a5e0-4abf-b6ca-803cc14dfa39][1985ce]] :: Rawls, [[id:c293c653-65b5-4f83-8218-d85c55f5b9c5][Justice as Fairness: Political not Metaphysical]], refines fairness and procedural liberalism
- [[id:0b6feb88-ff97-4708-b98a-0f9426838f8b][1989ce]] :: [[id:ec343638-d14d-4d67-b369-61a3588cb7d7][Judith Shklar]], [[id:e8e1cfa5-f61d-4d99-afa6-bffcad7e6628][The Liberalism of Fear]], describes procedural neutrality as a safeguard against cruelty
- [[id:0f249a60-e2cb-42fb-92f9-a34568490ddf][1990ce]] :: [[id:6ada433e-a6ec-4b21-af4c-0a3b7c01448f][Charles Larmore]], [[id:1b40ab4d-0577-453f-a9e8-858270099704][Political Liberalism]], argues pluralism should use [[id:d9a28e99-93a1-42e1-81ca-e406a568bd6c][neutral procedures]]
